# Topic: Water
T:Water
T:Reference
T:Category
T:Science
T:------------
T:What it is
T:Water is an
T:inorganic
T:compound
T:with the
T:chemical
T:formula H2O.
T:It is a
T:transparent,
T:tasteless,
T:odorless,
T:and nearly
T:colorless
T:chemical
T:substance.
T:It is the
T:main
T:constituent
T:of Earth's
T:streams,
T:lakes, and
T:oceans.
T:Water is
T:also the
T:fluid of all
T:known living
T:organisms,
T:in which it
T:acts as a
T:solvent.
T:Water, being
T:a polar
T:molecule,
T:undergoes
T:strong
T:intermolecul
T:hydrogen
T:bonding
T:which is a
T:large
T:contributor
T:to its
T:physical and
T:chemical
T:properties.
T:It is vital
T:for all
T:known forms
T:of life,
T:despite not
T:providing
T:food energy
T:or being an
T:organic
T:micronutrie.
T:Due to its
T:presence in
T:all
T:organisms,
T:its chemical
T:stability,
T:its
T:worldwide
T:abundance,
T:and its
T:strong
T:polarity
T:relative to
T:its small
T:molecular
T:size, water
T:is often
T:referred to
T:as the
T:"universal
T:solvent".
